  "summary": "Menlo Park PD revoked all existing ALPR data-sharing agreements on July 9 after a June audit found NCRIC and previously two out-of-state departments had conducted searches violating city policy and California law. The department now requires outside agencies to sign city-specific agreements with periodic audits; only five agencies have re-signed so far, down from 242. The story situates the changes amid broader Peninsula scrutiny of Flock cameras.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The department revoked all existing data-sharing agreements on July 9 and is requiring outside agencies seeking renewed access to Menlo Park\u2019s automated license plate reader (ALPR) data to sign new agreements and commit to periodic audits.",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "About 0.33%, or roughly 700 searches, did not comply with department policy based on the stated reason for the search.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "California law prohibits local police departments from sharing ALPR data with federal or out-of-state law enforcement agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "The department previously shared its ALPR data with 242 California agencies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 18
    }
  ],
